1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,745
Section seven.

2
00:00:02,025 --> 00:00:03,924
So far our SQL has been plain.

3
00:00:04,204 --> 00:00:09,835
Now we make it smarter: we install a package, use Jinja, write a macro, and meet analyses.

4
00:00:10,935 --> 00:00:14,050
A real problem: taxi trips have no trip ID.

5
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:16,122
Nothing identifies one trip.

6
00:00:16,402 --> 00:00:22,695
We need a primary key to test uniqueness, and later so an incremental model knows which rows to replace.

7
00:00:22,976 --> 00:00:24,981
We will build one from several columns.

8
00:00:26,081 --> 00:00:27,339
The packages page.

9
00:00:27,619 --> 00:00:33,785
A package is another dbt project you install into yours, usually for its macros and tests.

10
00:00:34,065 --> 00:00:37,756
You list packages in a YAML file and run dbt deps.

11
00:00:38,036 --> 00:00:41,897
The most used one is dbt utils, from dbt Labs.

12
00:00:42,997 --> 00:00:49,909
In the demo, the agent adds dbt utils with the latest compatible version and runs dbt deps.

13
00:00:50,189 --> 00:00:54,349
Afterwards we check the version on the dbt package hub ourselves.

14
00:00:54,629 --> 00:00:58,213
Version numbers are exactly what an AI can get slightly wrong.

15
00:00:59,313 --> 00:01:00,316
Three lines.

16
00:01:00,596 --> 00:01:04,094
Notice the version range: at least one point three, below two.

17
00:01:04,374 --> 00:01:06,657
We get fixes, but nothing that breaks us.

18
00:01:07,757 --> 00:01:09,656
Before using it, we understand it.

19
00:01:09,936 --> 00:01:15,312
In Ask mode: what does the surrogate key macro do, and which columns could identify a trip?

20
00:01:15,592 --> 00:01:22,717
It hashes several columns into one ID, and identical rows get the same key, which is how we will find duplicates.

21
00:01:23,817 --> 00:01:26,633
dbt models are SQL plus Jinja.

22
00:01:26,913 --> 00:01:30,284
Double curly braces print something, like ref and source.

23
00:01:30,564 --> 00:01:34,937
Curly brace and percent sign is logic: loops, ifs, and macros.

24
00:01:35,217 --> 00:01:42,513
Curly brace and hash is a comment. dbt renders the Jinja first, and the database only sees plain SQL.

25
00:01:43,613 --> 00:01:45,277
The Jinja and macros page.

26
00:01:45,557 --> 00:01:48,842
Jinja adds loops, variables and functions to SQL.

27
00:01:49,122 --> 00:01:52,642
A macro is a reusable function in the macros folder.

28
00:01:52,922 --> 00:01:56,271
And always check the compiled SQL to see what you produced.

29
00:01:57,372 --> 00:01:59,185
Our own macro, safe divide.

30
00:01:59,465 --> 00:02:01,428
Remember the trips with zero distance?

31
00:02:01,708 --> 00:02:03,926
Fare per mile would divide by zero.

32
00:02:04,206 --> 00:02:08,388
This macro returns empty instead of an error, and every model can use it.

33
00:02:09,488 --> 00:02:11,962
On the left, how we call the macro in a model.

34
00:02:12,242 --> 00:02:16,488
On the right, what dbt compiles it into: a plain case expression.

35
00:02:16,768 --> 00:02:18,816
The database never sees Jinja.

36
00:02:19,916 --> 00:02:22,774
Analyses are another block from the projects page.

37
00:02:23,054 --> 00:02:29,091
An analysis is a SQL file that dbt compiles but never runs, so no table is built.

38
00:02:29,372 --> 00:02:33,916
They are great for queries you want to keep in git, and for experimenting with Jinja.

39
00:02:35,016 --> 00:02:36,466
An analysis with a loop.

40
00:02:36,746 --> 00:02:40,138
For each payment type in the list, it writes one count column.

41
00:02:40,418 --> 00:02:43,576
Compile it, and the loop is replaced by real columns.

42
00:02:43,856 --> 00:02:46,543
But the list is hard-coded, which is not great.

43
00:02:47,644 --> 00:02:50,268
In the demo, the agent makes it data-driven.

44
00:02:50,548 --> 00:02:55,668
A dbt utils macro reads the payment types from the seed at compile time.

45
00:02:55,948 --> 00:02:59,852
Add a code to the CSV, and the SQL follows automatically.
